  Third question — what the rule set has to cover on day one. This is the YAGNI call, and unlike the previous two it's
  cheap to revise later, so I want your read rather than a guess.

  Today's only real rule is "username and password are both non-empty", and it returns a single error string. A reusable
  module normally needs at least: [38;5;153mrequired[39m, and a way to report [3mwhich field[0m failed rather than one flat message.

  - [1mMinimal[0m — [38;5;153mrequired[39m only, plus per-field errors. Covers the login form exactly and nothing more; you add rules when a
    form needs them.
  - [1mCommon set[0m — [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mmatches[39m (for confirm-password). Covers a typical
    signup/profile form without further work.
  - [1mCustom-function support[0m — either of the above, plus the schema accepting an arbitrary [38;5;153m(value, allValues) => string |
[39m    [38;5;153mnull[39m for one-off rules.

  [1mMy recommendation: minimal + custom-function support.[0m [38;5;153mrequired[39m is the only rule the repo actually needs today, and a
  custom-function escape hatch means the next form is never blocked waiting on me to add a built-in. Built-ins like
  [38;5;153memail[39m and [38;5;153mminLength[39m are a few lines each and can be added the moment a second form wants them — writing six validators
  speculatively is how a "reusable" module accumulates rules nobody calls.
